WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.390
Film Courage: And I think who you watch a
movie with in a theater plays a big role [in

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.390
how you see the movie].

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:07.819
I remember watching a film and the only other
person in the theater (and it was at night)

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:09.980
was a celebrity (I won’t say who it was).

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.630
But they were there to support the filmmaker
and that person was laughing and I found myself

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:15.630
laughing quite a bit, too.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.630
And then I thought “Wait a minute.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.859
Am I just trying to join in with this person?

00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:20.210
Is this why I am laughing?”

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.849
However, it was a great experience.

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:27.050
And it was surreal being in this huge theater
with the one famous person watching this film.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.130
Daniel Stamm: Was it Quentin Tarantino?

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.419
Film Courage: It was not.

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:30.419
I can say that.

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:34.900
Daniel Stamm: [Laughing] Because I have seen
him laugh through a movie of mine that wasn’t

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:35.900
supposed to be funny!

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.050
But he had such a good time.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.130
I don’t know if that’s a good thing or
bad thing?

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.410
But he was laughing from the first to the
last minute.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.560
And I was like “What does that mean?”

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.600
And you could tell that the rest of the audience
was confused because he has a very succinct

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.800
laugh.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.860
You can tell it’s Tarantino.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.420
Film Courage: Right…Daniel Stamm: So if
he thinks it’s funny then people are probably

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.700
wondering “Should I think it’s funny?”
and there is a weird energy throughout the

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.700
movie.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.370
And it’s just really interesting that it
probably has to do with that dynamic again,

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:11.640
the group dynamic…that there is a desire
to be on the same page with your fellow human

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.149
beings, kind of thing.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:18.310
And I think that is why everyone an audience
is kind of trying to have the same experience

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.750
and not be too far off from the tribe.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.250
That is maybe why comedies work so much better
with a big audience rather than a small audience

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.770
because there is less confusion.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.020
So it’s like everyone else is having fun
so I should have fun, too.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.569
And the subconscious tendency toward that.

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:41.430
Film Courage: And you never got an answer
as to why certain parts [of your movie] were

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.430
funny?

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:43.430
Daniel Stamm: To the Tarantino thing?

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.430
Film Courage: Right.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.320
Daniel Stamm: I talked to him afterwards and,
well you know how he talks [imitating high

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.659
energy] and all this stuff.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:53.549
But I don’t think I got a word in to ask
what he thought was funny.

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:59.090
Film Courage: I know sometimes if we go to
a theater to watch a movie and it’s usually

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:02.909
a mainstream film that is [at a chain theater]
in more of a sort of bedroom community and

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:07.229
these aren’t people in the industry, what
I’ll do is I’ll clap at the end [of the

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:08.880
movie] to see how many people will clap with
me.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.330
Because usually when you go to a Hollywood
film or something around this [Los Angeles]

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.230
area, people are all in the industry or they
know someone who is and they realize the hard

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.330
work that the people behind-the-scenes put
in….and they will clap at the end.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:24.379
I’m surprised (and sometimes David teases
me) at how many people don’t clap afterwards.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:26.620
And they just get out of their chairs, groan
and are like “Okay…What’s next?

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.030
Where do we go get cake and coffee after this?”

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.470
I find that interesting because [applause
at the end of a film] is so dependent on the

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.470
area in which you see it.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.489
Daniel Stamm: I’ve had that with flights.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:37.720
You know how some people clap after the plane
lands?

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.310
Film Courage: [Laughing] Yes….we made it!

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.840
Daniel Stamm: Right.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.730
When I first heard this I thought “Maybe
this is an American thing?”

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.519
because I think I was 35 years old when I
first set foot on a plane where people are

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:50.590
clapping and I was like “What did people
expect?

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.209
Are we celebrating this outcome?”

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:56.510
And it’s kind of the same (I think) with
a movie.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:02.689
That you expect the filmmakers that have had
a budget of millions of dollars and the audience

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:06.599
has just each paid their good money to see
that story.

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:11.530
They take it as a given that they are being
given a good time over those two hours.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:17.409
They feel like they shouldn’t have to celebrate
that they weren’t screwed over and robbed

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:18.629
of their money.

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:22.920
So I can understand the mindset of not clapping
after a movie.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.829
And I actually have that when the movie was
really good, I can’t clap or worse…. even

00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:31.030
talk to people afterwards.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.670
Like if you are really affected by something,
it goes so far.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:35.970
You’re in a different place.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.000
You’re not clapping.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.849
Like if you’re clapping, the movie hasn’t
100% gotten to you.

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:43.629
Maybe if it’s a comedy, that’s a different
thing.

00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:46.569
But if it’s Lars Von Trier's 'Breaking the
Waves.’

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:50.920
If that got you afterwards, you would not
clap.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.280
Because clapping means you are aware …. like
you were saying …. aware of the work that

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.230
has been done.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:02.939
But if the work has been done brilliantly,
you’re not even aware of the work that has

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:07.430
been done, you’re just flattened by the
story and the characters that you’ve just

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:08.430
experienced.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.599
You don’t think about directors, writers,
cinematographers, stunt men.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:17.060
All the stuff behind the scenes because you’re
so impressed by the narrative that you’ve

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.130
just experienced.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.940
I didn’t know I felt this way about clapping
until we just talked about it.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:26.730
But, that is maybe why I can understand why
people don’t clap.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:33.259
I don’t know if for one of my movies I would
rather have people clap or not clap?

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:37.270
I know for my first movie it was this fake
documentary about suicide.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.539
No one ever clapped after that movie.

00:04:39.539 --> 00:04:45.390
And I would have known that I would have failed
if they clapped after that movie.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:50.310
Whereas with the other movies, where it was
more of an upbeat ending or at least not that

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.320
dark, it probably would have been okay for
people to clap.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.320
Which they did’t either.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.370
Maybe I’m just making bad movies?

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.540
I don’t know what it is?

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.540
I don’t experience the clap.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:04:59.819
That is, outside of a festival audience.

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:04.530
I think at festivals because people assume
that some of the filmmakers are attending

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.099
and it is kind of polite to clap but I don’t
think I’ve ever experienced the audience

00:05:10.099 --> 00:05:11.319
was clapping at one of my movies?

00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:18.360
Film Courage: That would make an interesting
survey for artists that make films.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.370
Would you be offended if someone clapped after
your movie or would you take it as a compliment?

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.669
Daniel Stamm: It depends on the movie.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:26.250
It depends on the state you want to put people
into.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.230
Going back to David Fincher’s movie, at
the end of SEVEN, whether people are clapping

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.200
or not clapping and whether you want that.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.630
It probably depends on the story you were
just telling and the emotion you were hoping

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.930
to put the audience into.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:45.541
Fincher, it could be because we just had that
example earlier for SEVEN, you want to the

00:05:45.541 --> 00:05:51.610
audience to be so distressed and destroyed,
that he probably wouldn’t want anyone clapping

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:52.610
at the end of the movie.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.270
I think he would have known at the test screening
that something is wrong with this movie.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:00.580
Whereas there are probably other movies that
are more upbeat and have a different energy.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.970
Film Courage: True….and I am thinking back
to the [movies] that I’ve clapped at recently

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.620
and they were not….these sort of…melancholy
endings.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.940
So, that does make sense.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.000
Question For the Viewers: Do you ever clap
at the end of a movie?

